    "main_topic": [
      {
        "h_title": "Insight Thainess Episode 1: Mango with Sticky Rice",
        "main_title": "Insight Thainess Episode 1: Mango with Sticky Rice",
        "author": "TAT Newsroom",
        "language": "en",
        "level": 1,
        "primary_content": [
          {
            "text": "TAT Newsroom Short Documentary",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Theme: Warm emotional – Local Knowledge",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Location: Amphawa, Samut Songkhram",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "EPISODE SYNOPSIS",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "A chorus of cicadas is the rhythm and signal of summer, as well as the time for plenty of seasonal fruits. Amphawa, Samut Songkhram is one of the Thai communities that lives a simple lifestyle among various fruit orchards. The fruits that blossom and grow here are mostly coconuts, pomelos, lychees, and mangos.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Mango is a popular Thai homegrown fruit tree for shade, and we use the mango for consumption. The Thai people uses creativity to select what surrounds them to make as food. The Thais also have wisdom in wisely making good use of the fruit produce. They will carefully pick the old mangos from its tree to ripen for the sweet-sour taste, which the Thais believe that they give a refreshing fragrance and are best to be served with sticky rice and coconut milk. “Mango with Sticky Rice” is best served during summertime. This is the simplest happiness you can easily find in a Thai household.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          }
        ],
        "secondary_content": null,
        "table_content": null
      },
